WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.120
Jeremy Foley,&nbsp;
Filmmaker/Actor/Screenwriter:&nbsp;Actors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:09.680
we're surfers. We're just catching the wave. But&nbsp;
the filmmakers and the writers and the producers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:15.920
you've got to build the wave so&nbsp;
the actors can catch it and surf.

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:19.200
Film Courage: When did you stop&nbsp;
being an actor Jeremy and go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:23.280
from in front of the camera to behind&nbsp;
the camera as a writer/director?

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:30.640
Jeremy: I had continued my acting career all&nbsp;
throughout my teen years up through my early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:38.800
20's and I think the last film I did as an&nbsp;
actor I was probably 25 so over 10 years ago&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:50.480
and and before that though I'd still sort&nbsp;
of been acting a few times for for friends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:58.880
for projects where for instance actually the&nbsp;
last two films I did as an actor were really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:04.400
personal connections people that I knew&nbsp;
who were gonna be making a feature film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:09.600
and they were like listen we need one last&nbsp;
actor for this one last part and we think you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:14.560
can do it and I'd go in and these were again&nbsp;
these were friends and I would read for them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:22.000
and they'd be like great you're it and&nbsp;
we'd go off and we'd make the film so those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:29.440
that's actually my last two experiences as&nbsp;
an actor and then so that was through my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:38.240
early twenties and I think even during that I'd&nbsp;
been sort of experimenting with my friends and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:45.120
we had been sort of like still wasn't really&nbsp;
getting into it I was still kind of an actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:56.720
but like little by little we had started writing&nbsp;
we had started filming like small webisodes we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:02.400
had really been just just sort of practicing I&nbsp;
feel like people sort of like at least for me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:09.920
I had kind of grown up on sets and&nbsp;
so I'd seen people doing it but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:15.600
I hadn't done it myself and so these were my first&nbsp;
sort of like little experiments at like trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:21.280
do that myself shooting it myself and editing&nbsp;
it myself and writing it myself and sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:29.040
acting in it myself and sometimes my friends&nbsp;
would be acting in it and and so I think it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:35.840
around that time so really my early twenties&nbsp;
were when I stopped being an actor full-time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:45.840
and started doing my filmmaking and producing&nbsp;
full-time and I started out small with web series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:53.680
webisodes sketches and at that time there was&nbsp;
like a lot of stuff you could do like funny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.680 --> 00:03:00.720
or die or these places where you could submit&nbsp;
your comedy sketches or your webisode sketches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:06.240
and and hopefully you could kind of find&nbsp;
an audience or build an audience that way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:14.160
and that's kind of what we started doing and&nbsp;
then as I sort of progressed and I think a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:19.920
of people do that do it this way where they you&nbsp;
kind of get a little bit better and then you make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:26.000
the next one and it's just a little bit better and&nbsp;
then the next one's better until finally you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:32.480
at a place and this is this is where I was and&nbsp;
where I was with my my friends at that time where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:41.760
we were like okay all of this stuff that we've&nbsp;
done has been fun but I think now it's like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:47.680
there's a there's always I feel like a like a&nbsp;
a a switch or like you're you're ready to like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:54.560
go to the next level there's that evolution&nbsp;
of like this was everything I was doing and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.560 --> 00:04:03.840
now I'm going to one-up it and at that time&nbsp;
my friends and I started to make a web series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:09.040
and one of my friends one of my collaborators&nbsp;
still my one of my collaborators to this day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:14.320
which is why I'm talking about relationships&nbsp;
too is that the people that you meet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:22.000
and your friends and the people that you work&nbsp;
with are who and it's about knowing people right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:29.840
but one of my friends had this great&nbsp;
idea for a kind of a comedic web series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:37.520
and we could serialize it and break it into many&nbsp;
different parts and his ideas were about these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:44.000
mobsters from the east coast who have to come&nbsp;
out to the west coast and it's this sort of fish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:49.760
out of water comedy story and we were like great&nbsp;
that's a great idea let's go with that and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:59.200
became sort of the first real web series we did&nbsp;
called mobsters and I know it's still available&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:03.520
out there on funny or die or I'm sure I'm sure&nbsp;
people if they're interested can look it up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:12.640
but it was a lot of fun in and I want to say&nbsp;
that was the first time we really decided to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:22.160
step it up or really up our game and go to&nbsp;
that next level and really take it seriously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:31.440
and it really worked out well we had&nbsp;
an amazing experience with mobsters and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:37.600
we still like that idea to this day they're&nbsp;
your ideas are always they're always there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:41.920
in the back of your head even if you're not&nbsp;
actively working on something now I feel like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:50.000
nothing ever really goes away it's always there&nbsp;
so we we've always kind of got it in the wings but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:56.400
that was a great experience a great web series we&nbsp;
learned a lot we worked with a lot of excellent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:02.880
actors along the way if people want to look it up&nbsp;
they I mean we had excellent guest stars in some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:08.640
of these episodes we went to some excellent&nbsp;
film festivals with some of these webisodes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:16.800
and I think that was really eye opening to&nbsp;
all of us especially me to be like wow like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:24.240
we could actually make some really excellent&nbsp;
stuff if we just put our mind to it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:30.240
did you ever regret stepping away from acting what&nbsp;
was part of the allure of making your own stuff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:38.720
not having to go on auditions it's an interesting&nbsp;
question stepping away from acting for me

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:48.800
i kind of explained that like when I was&nbsp;
younger and when I was getting into acting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:58.080
for me it was the actors were the people that I&nbsp;
could see when you're a kid you're watching film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:03.120
or you're watching television and you see the&nbsp;
actors so I immediately thought well i've got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:09.760
to be an actor if I want to be in in movies and it&nbsp;
was this really interesting thing happened where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:17.680
when you're on set for instance using&nbsp;
dante's peak as an example you're on set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:25.200
and it was like a light bulb went&nbsp;
off I mean it really was like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:34.880
oh my gosh like I get it it's that there&nbsp;
are the actors and I'm one of those actors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:42.640
in this situation but then there's a director&nbsp;
and then there's camera operators and there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.640 --> 00:07:50.480
costumers and there's grips and there's all of&nbsp;
these people making these movies that I love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:59.920
and that the television shows that I love and it&nbsp;
was like oh whoa I get it like I'm i'm starting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:08.720
realize like you don't just have to be an actor&nbsp;
if you love film or television there are many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:18.640
ways to do this so for me I think&nbsp;
I being an actor was sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:27.520
almost a means to an end it sort of got&nbsp;
me involved in what I wanted to do but it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:35.280
wasn't necessarily the only thing I wanted&nbsp;
to do and I continued like that for years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:41.360
being an actor and so all throughout my teen&nbsp;
years again up into my early 20s I'd done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:51.040
numerous television shows guest spots I was series&nbsp;
regular on a show that ran for seasons I had done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:59.280
independent films starred in independent films&nbsp;
but that whole time you're watching and you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:04.800
learning and you're observing and you're seeing&nbsp;
like all this different stuff happen and all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:12.400
these different jobs that people are doing and&nbsp;
and and for me stepping away from acting was

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:20.720
you know I loved acting and still to this day&nbsp;
I love actors and I love what actors do and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:26.720
it's very easy for me because of my background&nbsp;
directing and producing and writing now it's very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:33.360
easy for me to relate to actors because I know&nbsp;
what they're going through but but I kind of see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:42.560
it as all just I always think of myself as just&nbsp;
a filmmaker and that can be producing and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:50.640
can be writing and that can be directing that can&nbsp;
even be acting I don't think specifically there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:57.920
one category or I have to fit only one&nbsp;
thing so stepping away from acting was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:05.760
it just felt like a natural progression&nbsp;
because what I wanted to do was actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:14.160
more storytelling focused and but still to this&nbsp;
day I love acting I love actors who knows maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:20.240
one day maybe i'll go back to it I don't know why&nbsp;
is being an actor easier than being a filmmaker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:28.080
I don't gosh I don't know if I don't know&nbsp;
being an actor is easier than being a filmmaker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:39.280
actors are really the talent that actors have is&nbsp;
so special it's really an amazing thing because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.280 --> 00:10:46.160
for actors and and I think I can say this I think&nbsp;
i've got enough sort of acting cred behind me that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.360 --> 00:10:53.680
and i've worked with some amazing actors I&nbsp;
really have but the talent that they have is so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.680 --> 00:10:59.840
special it's a it's it's there's&nbsp;
this incredible focus and presence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.040 --> 00:11:09.360
for an actor that they are just so in it they&nbsp;
have to be just so so right right there what I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:15.360
mean their mind can't be wandering and it can't&nbsp;
be thinking about something else and I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:24.000
for for filmmakers I think it's it's sort of&nbsp;
a different mind where your mind does have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:31.360
to sort of be able to focus on multiple&nbsp;
things at the same time whereas I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:43.120
acting you really have to be focused on one thing&nbsp;
so I wouldn't say it's any easier to do one or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:51.760
the other I just think that it's it's a completely&nbsp;
different sort of discipline and mindset and focus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.760 --> 00:12:02.240
that each one needs but but also it can really&nbsp;
depend now that because I have been both an actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.240 --> 00:12:08.000
and I have worked as a filmmaker and I gotta say&nbsp;
they're both tricky I mean they're both difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.640 --> 00:12:15.600
like being an actor is is its own thing you really&nbsp;
have to get used to auditioning you have to like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:22.560
auditioning you have to really feel comfortable&nbsp;
doing it as a filmmaker you have to get used to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:32.560
pitching you have to get used to there's a lot&nbsp;
of like personal meeting people you have to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.560 --> 00:12:41.840
used to calling people on the phone and like&nbsp;
really being sort of tenacious with that too um

00:12:43.920 --> 00:12:49.200
but I also see like moving ahead as&nbsp;
a filmmaker or getting a project off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:55.840
the ground as a filmmaker is also extremely&nbsp;
difficult I know this because i've tried it and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.840 --> 00:13:03.600
i've had so many projects that are almost&nbsp;
there and and don't quite for whatever reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:10.320
all the come to fruition the way I'd I would like&nbsp;
them to and that same thing can happen to an actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.440 --> 00:13:18.240
where you can almo you can you can get the&nbsp;
callback the third callback the fourth callback&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.240 --> 00:13:23.040
and you're so close and that doesn't really happen&nbsp;
so I just think they're completely different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.680 --> 00:13:30.480
disciplines I think but I think both of them are&nbsp;
have have their own challenges do you ever breathe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.480 --> 00:13:37.040
a sigh of relief and say oh now I don't have to&nbsp;
wait to be picked on I can I can be the picker

00:13:39.120 --> 00:13:47.920
you know I do breathe a sigh of relief sometimes&nbsp;
that I don't have to wait to be cast in something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.920 --> 00:13:56.560
that I can just go out and make it myself&nbsp;
and and I will say that that that freedom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.560 --> 00:14:04.240
is something that is so amazing about being a&nbsp;
filmmaker is that you can just go out and do it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.240 --> 00:14:11.280
you write it you put the crew together you produce&nbsp;
it you find the locations you cast the actors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.880 --> 00:14:18.800
you can you can work with the editor or you can&nbsp;
edit it yourself but yes absolutely having the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.800 --> 00:14:25.200
freedom to just go out and create is wonderful&nbsp;
and I think but I think on the same same token&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.200 --> 00:14:30.320
you're seeing a lot of actors now getting more&nbsp;
into that themselves I think a lot of actors are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.600 --> 00:14:38.480
sort of taking the reins for themselves&nbsp;
and shooting scenes for themselves or doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.480 --> 00:14:44.240
self-taping for themselves or even like i've seen&nbsp;
a lot of like sketches and stuff on like youtube&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:49.520
or things like that where it's just an actor&nbsp;
with their phone and they're kind of shooting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.080 --> 00:14:55.280
both both parts of the scene or they're shooting&nbsp;
over the shoulders and they're shooting they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.280 --> 00:15:01.360
making it look like they're two different&nbsp;
people so I think actors are starting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.080 --> 00:15:06.640
get into that indie filmmaker spirit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.360 --> 00:15:14.720
but but yeah it is it's an amazing freedom to be&nbsp;
able to just go out and start making stuff and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.720 --> 00:15:21.920
it's funny because I the last short film that my&nbsp;
wife and I made was called the faceless man and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.040 --> 00:15:31.040
I remember speaking to our actors and one of our&nbsp;
actors just saying actors we're we're surfers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.760 --> 00:15:39.440
we're just catching the wave but the filmmakers&nbsp;
and the writers and the producers you've got to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.440 --> 00:15:49.040
build the wave so the actors can catch it and&nbsp;
surf so I think they one hand needs the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.840 --> 00:16:00.400
one doesn't exist without the other but it's it&nbsp;
is nice to to just go out and do what you want.

